{"uuid":"BF896BF7-6EB2-53CB-98FB-29D748094C5D","name":"Ground Beef and Broccoli Bowls","serves":10,"duration":20,"cookingDuration":55,"defaultScale":1,"isPublicRecipe":false,"folderIDs":[],"ingredients":[{"uuid":"BB7EB451-B9D5-5FD1-931A-05A3664B8B25","order":0,"ingredient":{"uuid":"262FA192-8A65-5BFC-9E3B-E2ECE5EA6544","name":"packages ground beef (90/10), ~5 ⅔ lbs total"},"quantity":{"quantityType":"ITEM","amount":4}},{"uuid":"7BDCA4ED-7073-5D16-8727-F7A913D15EAE","order":1,"ingredient":{"uuid":"8D13D3B3-3E0B-5B0D-A302-18E440B8872F","name":"broccoli crowns (yields 940–1200 g roasted; crowns vary)"},"quantity":{"quantityType":"POUND","amount":5.5}},{"uuid":"A73D2223-1C37-5453-A6CB-CEC7457FC295","order":2,"ingredient":{"uuid":"F31E6FD3-7367-5A5C-9AB0-56C10012D3A6","name":"jasmine rice (dry)"},"quantity":{"quantityType":"CUP","amount":3.25}},{"uuid":"FCC25C68-F4D3-5ACE-A3F2-49BA93451829","order":3,"ingredient":{"uuid":"CDDA928A-80FE-541F-AA24-5E1B7E0F008B","name":"low-sodium soy sauce (see Notes if only regular is available)"},"quantity":{"quantityType":"CUP","amount":0.6667}},{"uuid":"DAF8A556-D665-566F-8173-ADBA7B9E6D54","order":4,"ingredient":{"uuid":"A73A8419-A0BE-58EC-9559-45E5C60B2EA1","name":"low-sodium beef broth"},"quantity":{"quantityType":"CUP","amount":0.75}},{"uuid":"C6773532-42E0-594A-8EB1-6C6959E4167D","order":5,"ingredient":{"uuid":"4AED6A1C-E459-507C-8EAE-C3C9C5AF0768","name":"cloves garlic, minced"},"quantity":{"quantityType":"ITEM","amount":9}},{"uuid":"F295F2C3-AE41-5B6E-BF10-B62D35E26B70","order":6,"ingredient":{"uuid":"AE113D8B-7FF5-58FD-A9A7-551E1CD9A627","name":"fresh ginger, minced (or 2 tsp ground)"},"quantity":{"quantityType":"TABLESPOON","amount":2}},{"uuid":"5CDDAF21-1E7D-50AB-A637-68296A327A1A","order":7,"ingredient":{"uuid":"C47F78CB-7AE7-5B2A-92A6-A3AF3974AE45","name":"toasted sesame oil"},"quantity":{"quantityType":"TABLESPOON","amount":2}},{"uuid":"F591A7BF-7CC2-5922-904B-EA741AD0DD4B","order":8,"ingredient":{"uuid":"C4F75C4B-510D-5A07-A463-F3DBB4C22529","name":"brown sugar"},"quantity":{"quantityType":"TABLESPOON","amount":2}},{"uuid":"E76CDC48-42E1-5B02-82F2-A55194549256","order":9,"ingredient":{"uuid":"802D9E26-C379-5A5B-96B6-22AA23C5CDE6","name":"rice vinegar (or cider vinegar)"},"quantity":{"quantityType":"TABLESPOON","amount":2}},{"uuid":"021AC940-C969-51F1-AE32-0CD759D1FF29","order":10,"ingredient":{"uuid":"BB81D452-3112-5D3A-863B-E5230CA1ABA4","name":"cornstarch + 4 tbsp cold water"},"quantity":{"quantityType":"TABLESPOON","amount":2}},{"uuid":"C612BFCE-35A1-58E9-A0A5-122EECAA00B3","order":11,"ingredient":{"uuid":"17512E44-FFC9-58CC-A7C6-29A79AE8A4BE","name":"red pepper flakes (optional)"},"quantity":{"quantityType":"TEASPOON","amount":0.5}},{"uuid":"3B1A59A1-2D53-5175-99F9-D13CBA758541","order":12,"ingredient":{"uuid":"5356374A-5373-540C-A35C-61C0290EAC12","name":"Olive oil, salt, pepper"}},{"uuid":"2B75DBC8-3FBC-54CD-BA7D-C7DB1E3EB6DE","order":13,"ingredient":{"uuid":"007E8F49-9D5E-57CA-BF9F-20879E37BDF7","name":"Scallions and sriracha (added fresh when eating)"}}],"steps":[{"uuid":"3A218E42-05B6-5ADB-A1CE-810AB58F8E96","order":0,"step":"Preheat the Ninja toaster oven to 400°F air roast.","isSection":false},{"uuid":"DD9B2A34-1E22-5D7F-BE67-A0746A96FA39","order":1,"step":"Rinse 3 ¼ cups jasmine rice 2–3 times until the water runs mostly clear. Add to the Instant Pot with 3 ¼ cups water (1:1).","isSection":false},{"uuid":"188CDBF3-7C6C-5881-8416-4B84C5177C61","order":2,"step":"Confirm the valve is set to SEALING. Pressure Cook (Manual) on High for 3 minutes, natural release 10 minutes, then quick release. Start this first — it takes ~10 minutes to come to pressure. Fluff and leave the lid off to cool.","isSection":false},{"uuid":"4ED024C5-1172-55CF-AAE2-EA4F4E46500B","order":3,"step":"Cut the broccoli into even florets, roughly 1 ½ inches. Uneven pieces mean the small ones burn before the large ones soften.","isSection":false},{"uuid":"4BB84A19-90C2-535B-AAB9-6EE09D4C0573","order":4,"step":"Toss the broccoli with olive oil, salt, and pepper. Spread in a single layer, nothing touching.","isSection":false},{"uuid":"24C56660-BA78-52E5-83E9-E43EE6AB8F16","order":5,"step":"Air roast at 400°F in the Ninja toaster oven for 12–15 minutes per batch, until the edges are slightly crispy. 5 ½ lbs takes 2 batches — start this while the beef browns.","isSection":false},{"uuid":"0C30160B-3CF6-5C58-A992-42C0E56C9370","order":6,"step":"Heat a large pan on the stovetop over medium-high. Add ~5 ⅔ lbs ground beef, breaking it up as it cooks. Brown 12–15 minutes until no pink remains. Work in 2 batches if the pan is crowded — crowding steams the meat instead of browning it.","isSection":false},{"uuid":"0CABB34E-A8E5-5ABE-A989-B54B0B24E822","order":7,"step":"Drain the fat thoroughly. 90/10 renders less than 80/20 but skipping this still makes the bowls greasy by day 3.","isSection":false},{"uuid":"6061DC93-E761-5F7D-A6A0-B461188419E5","order":8,"step":"In a saucepan, combine ⅔ cup low-sodium soy sauce, ¾ cup beef broth, the minced garlic and ginger, 2 tbsp brown sugar, 2 tbsp toasted sesame oil, and the red pepper flakes. Simmer 4–5 minutes to cook the raw edge off the garlic.","isSection":false},{"uuid":"1E7FC9D6-2922-5A9D-8424-4FA5073FD2F7","order":9,"step":"Taste the sauce before thickening. It should read savory and salty with only a background sweetness — if it tastes like a glaze, it has gone the wrong direction.","isSection":false},{"uuid":"3E189C15-23BB-52EC-9436-269E23901BAF","order":10,"step":"Whisk 2 tbsp cornstarch into 4 tbsp cold water, then stir the slurry into the simmering sauce. Cook 1–2 minutes until it coats the back of a spoon.","isSection":false},{"uuid":"8BB42436-AD1F-5F1D-9B2E-00CB2A4B40C2","order":11,"step":"Remove the sauce from the heat and stir in 2 tbsp rice vinegar. The acid goes in last — simmering it dulls the brightness that keeps this from tasting flat.","isSection":false},{"uuid":"3851E19B-EAFF-5B0E-B407-0791F1962957","order":12,"step":"Pour the sauce over the drained beef and stir to coat. Let it sit 5 minutes so the beef takes it up.","isSection":false},{"uuid":"1979F057-3A3B-5242-B220-9A8D2E0D4CB8","order":13,"step":"Cool the beef, rice, and broccoli 10–15 minutes before portioning. Sealing hot food causes condensation and sogginess.","isSection":false},{"uuid":"1B480C11-24A2-5255-8B4F-EAE36E2A4BB3","order":14,"step":"Assembly line across 10 open containers: 185 g beef, then 148 g rice, then 100 g broccoli. No separate sauce container — the sauce is already in the beef.","isSection":false},{"uuid":"1CCA2210-17A2-5169-863A-4F145BFF9649","order":15,"step":"Label with a dry-erase marker. 6 containers to the fridge, 4 to the freezer. Move freezer containers to the fridge the night before eating.","isSection":false}],"notes":"DIALED-IN — v2, last cooked 2026-08-23\n\nPER CONTAINER (10 total)\n  beef: 185 g\n  rice: 148 g\n  broccoli: 100 g\n  total: 433 g\n\nSTORAGE: 6 fridge, 4 freezer\nFreezer containers move to the fridge the night before eating.\n\nFRESH TOPPINGS — NEVER PACKED: sliced scallions\nAdded at eating time only.\n\nCooked 2026-08-23:\n\n  Beef       5.68 lbs raw → 1873 g   (yield 0.727)\n  Rice       3 ½ cups dry → 1592 g   (455 g per dry cup)\n  Broccoli   5.65 lbs raw →  966 g   (yield 0.377)\n\nv2 quantities come from that session. Three changes:\n\n**Beef is 4 packages, not 5 lbs.** Aldi sells lean ground beef in ~1.4 lb\npackages, so there is no combination that adds to 5. Four packages is about\n5 ⅔ lbs and lands 185 g per container.\n\n**Rice drops to 3 ¼ cups.** At 3 ½ it came out at 159 g per container against\na 140–150 target. Two sessions have now measured jasmine at ~455 g per dry\ncup, so 3 ¼ lands near 148.\n\n**Broccoli stays at 5 ½ lbs and the target drops to 100 g.** Two sessions\nmeasured crowns at 0.481 and 0.377 on near-identical raw weight — that spread\nis stalk length varying between crowns, not anything about the roasting.\n5 ½ lbs delivers 94–120 g per container across the whole observed range, which\nsits inside the 90–120 target either way, so the quantity is right and the\ntarget is set at the reliable end.\n\nThe sauce is deliberately savory rather than sweet. 2 tbsp of brown sugar\nacross 10 containers is about 3 g of added sugar each, against 17 g in the\nhoney garlic version that was rejected for tasting too sweet. Soy and sesame\ncarry it, the vinegar keeps it bright, and the sugar is there only to round\nthe salt. If it still reads flat after step 12, add vinegar before sugar.\n\nProjected sodium is ~750 mg, under the 800 ceiling but not by much, and it is\nalmost entirely the soy.\n\nSubstitutions, in order of how much they matter:\n\n**Low-sodium soy is load-bearing.** ⅔ cup of regular soy puts this at roughly\n1005 mg per container, well over the ceiling. If regular is all Aldi has, use\n½ cup and add ¼ cup more broth to keep the sauce volume — that lands near\n765 mg. Do not swap one for one.\n\n**Toasted sesame oil has no real substitute.** It is a finishing flavour, not\na cooking fat, and untoasted sesame oil is not the same thing. Aldi carries it\ninconsistently. Without it the sauce is still a good savoury garlic-ginger\nbeef, it just stops reading as sesame. Toasted sesame seeds stirred in at the\nend recover part of it. A small bottle is about five batches and keeps for\nmonths in the fridge, so it is worth sourcing once elsewhere.\n\n**The vinegar hardly matters.** Its job is acid against the salt and sugar.\nCider vinegar is the easy swap and Aldi always has it. White wine vinegar is\nclosest in profile. Distilled white works at 1 ½ tbsp rather than 2, since it\nis sharper than rice vinegar. Lemon juice works and reads brighter.\n\n**Ground ginger is fine** at 2 tsp in place of the fresh, and is the reliable\nAldi option.\n\nBroccoli softens slightly in the freezer but holds better than zucchini.\n\nSriracha can be packed straight into the containers — it is shelf-stable and\nvinegar-based, so it does not degrade or make anything soggy. Roughly 80 mg of\nsodium per teaspoon, which takes a container from ~750 mg to ~830 and just\nover the ceiling.\n\nScallions should not be packed. Sliced raw and left five days they go limp,\nand reheating cooks them into soft onion rather than a fresh garnish. Slicing\nthe whole bunch at prep and keeping it in a sealed container in the fridge\ngets the convenience without the wilt — sliced scallions hold about a week and\nsprinkling takes no knife at eating time.","neutritionalInfo":"Serving Size: 1 container,\nCalories: 685 kcal,\nProtein: 55 g,\nCarbohydrates: 52 g,\nFat: 27 g,\nFiber: 5 g,\nSodium: 750 mg,\nSugar: 3 g","sourceName":"ten-containers","webLink":"https://zjbecker32.github.io/ten-containers/recipes/ground-beef-broccoli/","tags":[],"images":[]}